theory.intro:
When an object is dropped into water, the water level rises. The **rise** in the water level equals the **volume** of the object.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[blue!30,opacity=0.45] (0,0) rectangle (2,1.1);
\draw (0,3.2)--(0,0)--(2,0)--(2,3.2);
\draw[blue] (0,1.1)--(2,1.1);
\node[above,font=\small] at (1,1.1) {start level};
\node[below,font=\small] at (1,0) {Before};
\fill[blue!30,opacity=0.45] (4,0) rectangle (6,1.9);
\draw (4,3.2)--(4,0)--(6,0)--(6,3.2);
\fill[gray,opacity=0.6] (5,0.55) circle (0.3);
\draw[blue] (4,1.9)--(6,1.9);
\node[above,font=\small] at (5,1.9) {new level};
\node[below,font=\small] at (5,0) {After};
\draw[dashed] (2,1.1)--(6.6,1.1);
\draw[<->] (6.6,1.1)--(6.6,1.9);
\node[right,font=\small] at (6.7,1.5) {rise};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
